{
  "assessment_mode": "sub-agent",
  "partner_verdicts": [
    {
      "partner": "visionary",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence is operating in one of the largest and most durable verticals in SMB software \u2014 field service management touches hundreds of thousands of businesses across HVAC, plumbing, electrical, landscaping, and adjacent trades in the US alone. The 18% month-over-month growth over four months on $40K MRR is the kind of early trajectory that, if it holds, compounds into a fund-returning outcome. And the fund's thesis explicitly covers Vertical SaaS, SMB software, and Future of Work/Field Operations \u2014 this is a direct hit on stated investment priorities.\n\nThat said, a visionary cannot commit without understanding what makes Cadence a category-defining company rather than a well-executed entrant into an already-competitive market. ServiceTitan, Jobber, Housecall Pro, and Workiz are all real, well-funded, and entrenched. The materials describe Cadence as scheduling, dispatch, crew management, and customer communication in one place \u2014 but that is the same description those incumbents would give. The differentiation story is absent. What is the unique insight that makes this team's version of field service software the one that wins? Is there an AI-native dispatch layer, a novel pricing model, a bottom-up PLG motion that incumbents cannot replicate, or a wedge into an underserved sub-vertical?\n\nThe team backgrounds \u2014 an ex-Stripe infrastructure engineer and an ex-Notion PM \u2014 signal strong technical and product-led-growth DNA, which is genuinely exciting for an SMB SaaS play. But neither founder has a lived domain experience in field services, which is the standard starting point for the most defensible vertical SaaS companies (the founder who was the plumber, the HVAC dispatcher, the landscaping operations manager). The visionary needs to understand what substitutes for that domain origin story.\n\nAt $333 implied ARPU (MRR divided by customer count), the current monetization is low for a B2B SaaS company. The visionary is not alarmed by this at seed \u2014 unit economics are fixable \u2014 but wants to know whether the pricing model compounds over time. Does Cadence have an expansion path: payments, financing for field service businesses, insurance, or a marketplace layer connecting contractors to leads? Without a compounding revenue architecture, a $16M pre-money valuation requires a very large number of SMBs at the current price point to return the fund.\n\nMore diligence on three fronts before committing: the differentiation thesis, the 10-year compounding vision, and the founder's unique insight into the problem."
    },
    {
      "partner": "operator",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence shows real execution evidence that earns attention: 120 paying customers at seed, 18% MoM growth sustained over a 4-month window, and a CAC of $1,200 against an ARPA of roughly $333/month translates to an approximately 4.5-month payback period at 80% gross margins. That is a capital-efficient acquisition motion \u2014 not something you see at this stage by accident. The 80% gross margins themselves confirm a clean software model with minimal infrastructure drag. These are operator-grade signals of a repeatable sales motion working right now.\n\nHowever, the single structural concern that prevents a conviction vote is the team composition. Both co-founders come from product and engineering backgrounds \u2014 ex-Stripe infrastructure and ex-Notion product management. Neither profile maps to a commercial operator who has run SMB field-service sales. For a vertical SaaS targeting SMB service businesses, the GTM motion is the hard part: these customers respond to referrals, trade associations, industry-specific channels, and high-touch onboarding. There is no disclosed evidence of what acquisition channel is actually driving the 120-customer base or whether that channel is repeatable and scalable beyond word-of-mouth.\n\nI also have no visibility into churn or net dollar retention. A 4.5-month payback only matters if customers stay. At $333/month ARPA, a single churned SMB wipes out 3-4 months of gross profit contribution. Without cohort data, the LTV side of the unit economics equation is ungrounded. I want to see retention curves before voting to write a check. The ask of $4M at $16M pre-money is a 33x trailing ARR multiple \u2014 defensible only if growth is durable and the team can execute the GTM build-out without a commercial hire already in place or a concrete plan to make one."
    },
    {
      "partner": "analyst",
      "verdict": "more_diligence",
      "rationale": "Cadence shows a compelling surface-level unit economics profile \u2014 80% gross margins are at the high end for vertical SaaS, and a CAC of $1,200 against $333/month ARPA (implied from $40K MRR across 120 customers) yields a CAC payback of approximately 4.5 months, which is exceptional by any benchmark. The 18% month-over-month growth average over four months is strong and, if sustained, annualizes to roughly 7x revenue growth. These are not trivial signals at seed.\n\nHowever, the materials disclose none of the data I need to underwrite a conviction investment. There is no churn rate, no cohort retention curve, no net dollar retention figure, and no gross revenue retention \u2014 without these, the LTV side of the LTV/CAC equation is entirely unanchored. For an SMB-focused product in field services, which is a historically high-churn segment, this omission is not neutral: SMB SaaS benchmarks typically see 20-30% annual churn, which would compress LTV significantly. The absence of retention disclosure is absence-as-evidence in a segment where churn is the primary financial risk.\n\nThe valuation at $16M pre-money on $480K implied ARR equates to approximately 33x ARR \u2014 aggressive for seed stage, particularly without a disclosed funding history, burn rate, or runway figure. I cannot assess whether the $4M raise is capital-efficient without knowing current burn. The materials also provide no cohort data, no revenue concentration breakdown, and no path to Series A metrics defined. The team has no commercial co-founder, which for a $4M GTM-heavy raise into the SMB segment is a material gap. I need retention curves, burn/runway, and a customer concentration breakdown before I can move to invest."
    }
  ],
  "debate_sections": [
